//
//  1. User picks a Mode (build, plan, general) at session start via config or CLI.
//     The Mode is the single routing signal — the harness never classifies the
//     user's prompt to pick a lane automatically.
//
//  2. `promptRouting.go` resolves the Mode for this turn via `currentMode()` and
//     wraps it into a `promptExecutionPlan{mode: ...}`.
//
//  3. `RunPromptOnce` (prompt_routing.go) calls `runStandardPromptOnce` which:
//     - resolves workspace paths mentioned in the prompt
//     - appends the user message
//     - delegates to `runAgentTurn` (via the agent package)
//
//  4. The agent `Runner` (agent/runner.go) drives the actual tool loop:
//     - applies `PermissionPolicy` to gate tool calls
//     - applies `DiscoveryRedirect` to redirect generic tools to language-specific ones
//     - enforces budget limits (max tool steps, model calls, context size, duration)
//     - tracks identical call runs to prevent infinite loops
//     - emits budget ledger, readiness checks, and evidence delta logs
//
//  5. Child tasks (via the "task" tool) are routed through `runChildTask` which
//     dispatches to either `workbranch_write` (write mode) or `read_only` (explore
//     mode) based on the mode field in the tool request.
//
//  6. The workerruntime package provides RoleSpec definitions that map role names
//     to permission profiles, allowed/forbidden tools, required evidence, readiness
//     gates, search policies, verification contracts, and stop conditions.
//
//  7. The Runner's `CompletionGate` callback (set by the app) determines whether
//     the model's answer is acceptable or needs retry.
